Overlay patch 0056: your OWN items, in first person (VR R9 item 5, the user's
addendum — "make sure we can see the 3 shells going around ourself").

the user has never once seen his own triple shells in the headset, and compared
ours unfavourably against RaYRoD's MK64 VR on exactly this. Nothing culls them:
0048 already stands the angle cull down in VR, `is_within_render_distance`
passes 0 as the minimum distance so there is no near reject at all, and 0054
deliberately skips only the local player's kart QUAD. They are drawn. They are
just drawn in a way that cannot be seen from inside the kart, for two
independent reasons, and both are fixed here.

  1. THE BILLBOARD MODEL IS WRONG AT COCKPIT RANGE (src/racing/actors.c).

     `sBillBoardMtx` is ONE shared Y rotation, built once per frame for every
     course billboard, and overlay 0051 re-keyed it to the composed EYE YAW.
     That makes every quad's normal parallel to the eye's FORWARD vector — it
     faces the view DIRECTION, not the object→eye direction. For a tree 2000
     units away those are the same to within a degree. For a shell orbiting
     EIGHT GAME UNITS from an eye that is sitting inside the kart, the
     object→eye direction is 60-90 degrees off forward, so the quad is
     violently foreshortened and goes EXACTLY EDGE-ON (zero width) whenever the
     shell is at your 3 or 9 o'clock. Worse, the world pass runs with
     G_CULL_BACK and the actor pass never clears it, so past 90 degrees the
     quad is not merely thin — it is culled outright.

     Note the asymmetry inside 0051 that this repairs: its item 1 re-keyed the
     POSITIONAL kart billboards to the eye POSITION
     (`atan2s(pos - gSohVREyePosGame)`, player_controller.c), while its item 2
     gave sBillBoardMtx only an eye-YAW re-key. Every sBillBoardMtx consumer
     therefore got the far-field fix and not the near-field one — and your own
     items are the only sBillBoardMtx objects that are ever at cockpit range,
     which is precisely why nothing else ever exposed it.

     `render_actor_shell` builds a PER-OBJECT facing from the eye POSITION when
     the shell is inside SOH_VR_NEARFIELD units of the eye, into a LOCAL matrix
     — the caller's `Mat4` is the shared sBillBoardMtx and writing the rotation
     back into it would poison every actor drawn after this one. Distant shells
     keep the shared matrix and its cost, exactly as before.

REV 2 (VR R10 item 4) — THE CUSTOM ORBIT IS GONE.

rev1 also widened the local player's triple-shell orbit to 40 units and raised
it to +6 for VR first person, because MK64's authored orbit (radius 8, ~1.75
below the kart origin, drawn ~3 lower again) puts the ring under the driver's
lap when the eye is 12 units up. That was sim-tuned, and the headset's verdict
on 1.0.0.6 was unambiguous: "way too high off the ground, rotates WAY too fast,
faint — just make them look normally how they do in the game in regular play."

Both complaints are the same number. `shell->rotAngle` advances by the game's
own `parent->rotVelocity` either way, so the ANGULAR speed was never changed —
but a 40-unit radius sweeps five times the arc length of an 8-unit one at the
same angular rate, and a billboard authored for a chase camera 200 units back
is enormous at 40. Widening the ring made it look fast and made it look wrong,
and it also widened the shells' COLLISION radius, which was always the trade.

So rev2 deletes the custom placement outright. MK64's own orbit — position,
radius, height, angular speed and opacity — renders exactly as it does in flat
play, and the ONLY thing this patch still does is fix 1 below: face the quad at
the composed eye when it is close enough for the shared view-direction matrix
to be visibly wrong. That was the genuine bug; the orbit was a guess.

The held/trailing item is deliberately NOT moved: a held shell sits ~11.75
units behind the kart, which is behind your head, and behind is where a held
item belongs. Fix 1 makes it face you properly when you look back or catch it
in the rear-view pane.


--- a/src/racing/actors.c
+++ b/src/racing/actors.c
@@ -775,10 +775,23 @@
 
 #include "actors/kiwano_fruit/render.inc.c"
 
+#ifdef __IOS__
+// SOH_IOS (overlay 0056, VR R9 item 5): how close an item has to be to the
+// eye before the SHARED view-direction billboard stops being a good enough
+// approximation of a position-facing one. 8 units is the triple-shell
+// orbit; 200 covers anything sharing the cockpit with you without paying
+// for a per-object matrix on the hundreds of distant course billboards.
+#define SOH_VR_NEARFIELD 200.0f
+#endif
+
 void render_actor_shell(Camera* camera, Mat4 matrix, struct ShellActor* shell) {
     u16 index;
     char* phi_t3;
     bool reverseShell = false;
+#ifdef __IOS__
+    Mat4 sohNearMtx;
+    Mat4* sohUseMtx = (Mat4*) &matrix[0];
+#endif
 
     size_t actorIdx = CM_FindActorIndex((struct Actor*)shell);
     if (-1 == actorIdx) {
@@ -826,7 +839,40 @@
     matrix[3][1] = (shell->pos[1] - shell->boundingBoxSize) + 1.0f;
     matrix[3][2] = shell->pos[2];
 
+#ifdef __IOS__
+    // SOH_IOS (overlay 0056, VR R9 item 5): a PER-OBJECT facing for items
+    // sharing the cockpit with you. The caller's matrix is the SHARED
+    // sBillBoardMtx, whose single Y rotation faces the eye's forward
+    // DIRECTION — correct to within a degree for a tree 2000 units away,
+    // and exactly edge-on for a shell orbiting 8 units from your head. So
+    // the near-field case builds its own yaw from the eye POSITION, the
+    // same rule overlay 0051 already applies to the kart billboards, into a
+    // LOCAL copy — writing the rotation back into the shared matrix would
+    // poison every actor drawn after this one.
+    if (gSohVREyeKey) {
+        f32 sohDx = shell->pos[0] - gSohVREyePosGame[0];
+        f32 sohDz = shell->pos[2] - gSohVREyePosGame[2];
+        if ((sohDx * sohDx + sohDz * sohDz) < (SOH_VR_NEARFIELD * SOH_VR_NEARFIELD)) {
+            s16 sohYaw = atan2s(sohDx, sohDz);
+            f32 sohS = sins(sohYaw - 0x8000);
+            f32 sohC = coss(sohYaw - 0x8000);
+            s32 sohR, sohCc;
+            for (sohR = 0; sohR < 4; sohR++) {
+                for (sohCc = 0; sohCc < 4; sohCc++) {
+                    sohNearMtx[sohR][sohCc] = matrix[sohR][sohCc];
+                }
+            }
+            sohNearMtx[0][0] = sohC;
+            sohNearMtx[0][2] = -sohS;
+            sohNearMtx[2][0] = sohS;
+            sohNearMtx[2][2] = sohC;
+            sohUseMtx = &sohNearMtx;
+        }
+    }
+    maxObjectsReached = render_set_position(*sohUseMtx, 0) == 0;
+#else
     maxObjectsReached = render_set_position(matrix, 0) == 0;
+#endif
     if (maxObjectsReached) {
         return;
     }
